course: dictionary
lesson: 10331
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. The word train is a good example. Read the words around it to find out which meaning fits: «The train left the station on time.» Here train is a thing you can ride, a line of railway carriages pulled by an engine.
explain: «Sam will train every evening for the race.» Here train means to practise so you get better at something, like a sport. Ask yourself which meaning fits the words around it, then try that meaning in the sentence to check it makes sense.
explain: «Ratings are decided by trained examiners who follow published rules.» Trained means taught a skill, so a trained examiner has learned how to do the job well. The same word can be a noun, a verb, or part of a phrase, so always check the clues.
fact: Train can be a noun, a thing: a train is a line of railway carriages pulled by an engine along tracks.
fact: Train can be a verb, an action: to train means to practise to get better at something, or to teach a person or animal a skill.
fact: «A train of thought» means a line of ideas, and «the train of a long dress» means the part that trails on the floor.
fact: «Picasso was trained from a young child» means he was taught to draw from a young age.
fact: Train, trains, trained and training are all forms of the same word, so look at the ending and the sentence to tell what is going on.
exercise: «The train left the station on time.» What does train mean here? | The part of a dress that trails on the floor | A line of ideas | *A line of railway carriages pulled by an engine | To practise to get better at something
exercise: «Foster carers are checked and trained, and social workers visit to make sure children are safe.» What does trained mean here? | *Taught a skill | Lost in thought | Practised a sport every day | Pulled along tracks
exercise: «Foster carers are checked and trained, and social workers visit.» Find and copy one word that means taught a skill. | checked | carers | social | *trained
test: «Sleep isn't a break from training, it is the part of training where the body does its building.» What does training mean here? | A line of ideas | *Practising to get better at a skill | A railway line with carriages | The part of a dress that trails
test: Ella says «The train of his dress trailed on the floor.» What is the train? | A line of ideas | *The part of a dress that trails on the floor | A line of railway carriages | To practise a sport
exam: Which sentence uses train to mean a line of ideas? | Sam will train for the race every day | The train of the gown swept across the floor | The train left the station at nine | *She lost her train of thought when the phone rang
